  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 734 PM MDT Tue Sep 15 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nDue to an upper-level trough clipping north MT, there will be areas \nof light rain showers this evening through the early hours of \nWednesday morning. However, showers are expected to be limited to \nnorth central MT and the north Rockies. \n\nWednesday will be a break between the two upper level troughs as \nweak upper level ridging will be present. As such, conditions will \nbe dry and mild for the majority of the state.\n\nDuring Tuesday night and Wednesday, the trough that has been sitting \nstagnant over the PNW will develop a closed off low and begin \nshifting eastward. This eastward movement will cause upper level SW \nflow to develop over MT on Wednesday. The upper level SW flow is \nexpected to continue through Saturday as the upper level low \ncontinues its eastward track across the state. \n\nAlthough upper level SW flow is expected to develop Wednesday, \nwarmer weather and the potential for showers/thunderstorms are \nexpected to hold off until Thursday. Looking at Thursday and beyond, \nwarmer weather and the potential for showers/thunderstorms will \ncontinue through Friday night. By midday Saturday, the potential for \nshowers/thunderstorms will have ended as the low makes continues its \neastward track across the state. \n\nBeginning Sunday, a period of warmer and drier conditions will \ndevelop and is expected to continue into the beginning of the \nfollowing week. - ESH\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nThunderstorms on Thursday and Friday...\n\nCAPE and shear per ECMWF EFIs will climb to between 0.6 and 0.75 \nalong and south of the I-90 corridor on both Thursday and Friday, \nwith Friday having the greatest coverage of EFIs in excess of 0.6 \nacross the aforementioned region. BUFKIT soundings across Southwest \nMontana indicate high cloud bases in excess of 10kft, with very dry \nair beneath these bases (i.e. inverted-Vs). This dry air combined \nwith climatologically unusual, with respect to September \nclimatology), instability and shear would lend to the idea that a \nfew thunderstorms that develop on either day would be capable of \nproducing strong and erratic outflow winds. BUFKIT soundings also \nindicate tall and skinny CAPE profiles across Southwest and portions \nof Central Montana, which when considered against PWATs of 2-3 \nstandard deviations above normal (or between 0.75\" to 1\") suggests \nthe potential for some thunderstorms to produce heavy rainfall. - \nMoldan",
